    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p><strong>Do under desk elliptical machines work?</strong> Yes, but "under desk exercise machine" covers several very different devices, and they do not all work the same way. A manual seated elliptical makes your legs do the work. A motorized leg exerciser does the work for you. A desk bike moves your legs in a circle, and a mini stepper is meant to be used standing.</p>
<p>Each one works for some goals and not for others. This guide compares the four main types on calorie burn, muscle use, comfort, noise and who they suit, so you can pick the one that will actually deliver what you want.</p>
<p>For the research behind seated movement in general, see our <a href="/does-an-under-desk-elliptical-work/">evidence-based answer to whether under desk ellipticals work</a>. This page is about choosing the right type.</p>

<h2>Do Under Desk Elliptical Machines Work? The Four Types at a Glance</h2>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Who does the work</th><th>Calorie burn</th><th>Best for</th><th>Verdict</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Manual seated elliptical</td><td>You</td><td>Light to moderate</td><td>Most desk workers</td><td>Works</td></tr>
<tr><td>Motorized / passive leg exerciser</td><td>The motor (you can pedal along)</td><td>Very low in passive mode</td><td>Limited mobility, circulation comfort</td><td>Works for comfort, not fitness</td></tr>
<tr><td>Under desk bike</td><td>You</td><td>Light to moderate, higher with resistance</td><td>People who want more resistance</td><td>Works, harder on knees for some</td></tr>
<tr><td>Mini stepper</td><td>You, standing</td><td>Moderate</td><td>Standing desk users, short breaks</td><td>Works, but not under a seated desk</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>

<h2>Manual Under Desk Ellipticals</h2>
<p>A manual seated elliptical has two foot pedals connected to a flywheel, usually with magnetic resistance you set with a dial. Your feet travel in a smooth oval path. You push the pedals; nothing moves unless you do.</p>
<h3>How well they work</h3>
<p>Manual models give you the most "exercise" of the seated options because your muscles do all the work. At light effort, they roughly add 50 extra calories an hour over sitting; at a brisk pace with resistance, closer to 100 or more for an average-size adult. Your quads, hamstrings, glutes and calves all contribute.</p>
<p>The elliptical motion keeps your knees in a smaller range than a bike, which many owners find more comfortable for long sessions. Good magnetic models are quiet enough for video calls.</p>
<h3>Where they fall short</h3>
<p>Resistance tops out lower than a desk bike on most compact models, and you can slide in your chair if the machine is not anchored. Budget units with friction resistance can get noisy.</p>
[[product:cubii-jr2|1]]
<p>The [[amazon:deskcycle-ellipse|DeskCycle Ellipse]] is the low-profile alternative for tight desk clearance, and the [[amazon:sunny-sf-e3872|Sunny Health &amp; Fitness]] model is the budget option. Our <a href="/manual-under-desk-elliptical/">manual under desk elliptical guide</a> covers more picks.</p>

<h2>Motorized and Passive Leg Exercisers</h2>
<p>Motorized under desk ellipticals use an electric motor to move the pedals at a set speed, usually controlled with a remote. You rest your feet on the pedals and your legs move along. Many models also let you switch the motor off and pedal manually, or pedal along with the motor for a little more effort.</p>
<h3>Do leg exercisers work?</h3>
<p>For gentle, continuous movement, yes. They keep your knees, hips and ankles moving through a range of motion without needing strength or stamina, which is why they are popular with seniors and people recovering from injury. Owners often say their legs feel less stiff and heavy.</p>
<p>For fitness or weight loss, mostly no. When the motor does the work, your muscles barely contract, so the calorie burn is only slightly above sitting. Treat passive mode as a comfort and mobility tool.</p>
<div class="callout warn"><p>If you are using a motorized leg exerciser for a medical reason, such as circulation, swelling or rehab after surgery, ask your doctor or physical therapist which settings are safe for you.</p></div>
[[product:ancheer|2]]
<p>The [[amazon:lifepro-autoglide|Lifepro AutoGlide]] and the [[amazon:yosuda-electric|YOSUDA electric]] model are other powered options; the YOSUDA can also be used manually. See our full list of the <a href="/best-motorized-under-desk-elliptical/">best motorized under desk ellipticals</a>.</p>

<h3>Hybrid models: the middle ground</h3>
<p>Some electric machines let you switch between motor-driven and manual pedaling. These suit people whose energy varies: on a good day you pedal against resistance, and on a sore or tired day you let the motor carry the motion. The trade-off is weight, a power cord to route under your desk, and a higher price than a basic manual unit. If you are buying for an older parent or someone in rehab, a hybrid is often the most flexible choice because it grows with them as strength returns.</p>

<h2>Under Desk Bikes</h2>
<p>An under desk bike, also called a pedal exerciser or desk cycle, works like a stationary bike without the seat. Your feet move in a circle instead of an oval.</p>
<h3>How well they work</h3>
<p>Desk bikes often offer a wider resistance range than compact ellipticals, so they can deliver a harder workout when you want one. The DeskCycle 2 is known for a low pedal height and a broad resistance range. For people who want to push into moderate effort during calls or breaks, a desk bike can work slightly better than a light elliptical.</p>
<h3>Where they fall short</h3>
<p>The circular motion lifts your knees higher at the top of each stroke, which makes desk clearance harder and can be less comfortable for sensitive knees. Some people also find the pedaling motion more noticeable while typing.</p>
[[product:deskcycle-2|3]]
<p>For a full head-to-head, read our <a href="/under-desk-elliptical-vs-bike/">under desk elliptical vs bike comparison</a>.</p>

<h2>Mini Steppers and Compact Striders</h2>
<p>Mini steppers have two pedals that move up and down as you shift your weight. They are made to be used standing, and some come with resistance bands for your arms. Compact striders such as the [[amazon:stamina-inmotion-e1000|Stamina InMotion E1000]] are similar: built for standing use, though they can also be used seated.</p>
<h3>How well they work</h3>
<p>Because you are standing and moving your body weight, steppers can reach a higher effort level than seated machines, and they add some balance and bone-loading benefit. They work well for short, focused breaks at a standing desk.</p>
<h3>Where they fall short</h3>
<p>You cannot use a mini stepper under a normal seated desk, and most people cannot type comfortably while stepping. They are also louder than a magnetic seated elliptical, and you need something to hold for balance. You can [[search:mini stepper|browse mini steppers on Amazon]] if you have a standing desk. Our <a href="/stamina-inmotion-review/">Stamina InMotion review</a> covers the strider option in detail.</p>

<h2>Do Foot Ellipticals Work? Mini vs Full-Size Seated Models</h2>
<p>Within the manual category there is a big spread in size. "Foot ellipticals" and mini models such as the [[amazon:cubii-go|Cubii GO]] are small and light, with a shorter stride and lighter resistance. Full-size seated ellipticals have a longer stride, a heavier flywheel and more resistance levels.</p>
<p>Both work for breaking up sitting. The difference shows up in how much effort you can put in:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Mini foot ellipticals:</strong> easy to move between rooms and store, fit under low desks, and are fine for gentle ankle and calf movement. They feel too easy for many people within a few weeks.</li>
<li><strong>Full-size seated ellipticals:</strong> smoother at higher speeds, stay planted better, and give you room to raise resistance as you get fitter. They take more floor space and are heavier to carry.</li>
</ul>
<p>If your main goal is calories or leg endurance, a full-size model works better. If it is portability or very limited space, a mini model is the more practical choice.</p>

<h2>Common Reasons a Desk Machine Seems Not to Work</h2>
<p>When owners say their under desk elliptical machines don't work, the cause is often a mismatch between the type and the goal. A passive motorized unit bought for weight loss will disappoint. A mini foot elliptical bought for a hard workout will feel pointless after a month. A desk bike under a low desk will knock your knees and end up in storage. Matching the type to your goal, as in the table below, avoids most of these problems.</p>

<h2>Which Under Desk Elliptical Machines Work Best for Your Goal?</h2>
